<w:document xmlns:w="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/wordprocessingml/2006/main" xmlns:m="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/math" xmlns:r="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships" xmlns:wp="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/wordprocessingDrawing" xmlns:a="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main" xmlns:pic="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/picture" xmlns:ct="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/content-types" xmlns:rel="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/relationships"><w:body><w:p><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Title"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">The Conformal Classifier Two-Sample Test</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Subtitle"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">UAT Fixture</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Subtitle"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">January 2, 2026</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Abstract"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">We study the </w:t></w:r><m:oMath><m:r><m:rPr><m:scr m:val="script"/><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t xml:space="preserve">M</m:t></m:r></m:oMath><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">-closed validation of a generative model </w:t></w:r><m:oMath><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">q</m:t></m:r></m:oMath><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> against a target </w:t></w:r><m:oMath><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">p</m:t></m:r></m:oMath><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> over the space </w:t></w:r><m:oMath><m:r><m:rPr><m:scr m:val="script"/><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t xml:space="preserve">X</m:t></m:r></m:oMath><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">. Conformal calibration turns a weak plug-in classifier into a powerful two-sample test with finite-sample type-I control.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Heading1"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="3"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Introduction</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Normal"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">A popular tool for evaluating generative models is the classifier two-sample test (C2ST). It reframes distributional comparison between </w:t></w:r><m:oMath><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">p</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">(</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">x</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">)</m:t></m:r></m:oMath><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> and </w:t></w:r><m:oMath><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">q</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">(</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">x</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">)</m:t></m:r></m:oMath><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> as a classification task over the space </w:t></w:r><m:oMath><m:r><m:rPr><m:scr m:val="script"/><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t xml:space="preserve">X</m:t></m:r></m:oMath><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">, with the Bayes-optimal classifier bisecting </w:t></w:r><m:oMath><m:sSup><m:e><m:r><m:rPr><m:nor/><m:scr m:val="double-struck"/><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t xml:space="preserve">R</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sup><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">2</m:t></m:r></m:sup></m:sSup></m:oMath><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> between the two means.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Heading4"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Summary of contributions.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Normal"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">We introduce the Conformal C2ST and give a robustness theory for weak classifiers. We show that conformal calibration decouples type-I error control from discriminative accuracy.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Heading4"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">A toy example.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Normal"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Consider </w:t></w:r><m:oMath><m:r><m:rPr><m:scr m:val="script"/><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t xml:space="preserve">N</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">(</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="b"/></m:rPr><m:t xml:space="preserve">0</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">,</m:t></m:r><m:sSub><m:e><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">I</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sub><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">2</m:t></m:r></m:sub></m:sSub><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">)</m:t></m:r></m:oMath><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> versus a mean shift, so that </w:t></w:r><m:oMath><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">q</m:t></m:r></m:oMath><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> is </w:t></w:r><m:oMath><m:r><m:rPr><m:scr m:val="script"/><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t xml:space="preserve">N</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">(</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">[</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">0.5</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">,</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">0</m:t></m:r><m:sSup><m:e><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">]</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sup><m:r><m:rPr><m:nor/></m:rPr><m:t xml:space="preserve">⊤</m:t></m:r></m:sup></m:sSup><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">,</m:t></m:r><m:sSub><m:e><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">I</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sub><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">2</m:t></m:r></m:sub></m:sSub><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">)</m:t></m:r></m:oMath><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">. The rank statistic</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Normal"/></w:pPr><m:oMathPara><m:oMath><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">r</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:nor/></m:rPr><m:t xml:space="preserve">=</m:t></m:r><m:nary><m:naryPr><m:chr m:val="∑"/><m:limLoc m:val="undOvr"/></m:naryPr><m:sub><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">i</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:nor/></m:rPr><m:t xml:space="preserve">=</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">1</m:t></m:r></m:sub><m:sup><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">M</m:t></m:r></m:sup><m:e><m:r><m:rPr><m:nor/><m:scr m:val="double-struck"/><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t xml:space="preserve">I</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t xml:space="preserve">{</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:nor/></m:rPr><m:t xml:space="preserve">θ</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:nor/></m:rPr><m:t xml:space="prese